      Only `PASSED` and `FAILED` outcomes are treated as completed evidence; `NETWORK_BLOCKED`, `TIMED_OUT`, and `NOT_STARTED` are left as non-completed outcomes, so evidence lacking a completed run can still be surfaced without being classified at the static tier in the review output.
      src/acceptance/execution/outcome.py#0
    [other] static-tier-for-unrun-evidence/missing-outcome-falls-through
      If a requested test never appears in the report, `run_tests` synthesizes a `NOT_STARTED` outcome, but there is no code here that maps that outcome to the static tier in the review output; the criterion could fail if downstream output treats the synthesized result as ordinary evidence instead of static-tier evidence.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
      src/acceptance/execution/outcome.py#0

  review-finishes-normally
    [other] review-finishes-normally/sandbox-errors-propagate
      run_tests can still return an error-like result when the subprocess setup or execution fails, because the broad exception handler turns any exception into an all-not-started result with a failure reason instead of guaranteeing a normal completion path for the review.
