  only-named-tests-run
    [missing_case] only-named-tests-run/empty-test-list-runs-full-suite
      When `test_ids` is empty, `run_tests` returns `SandboxRunResult(outcomes=[])` instead of refusing the request, so the caller can still reach pytest with no node ids and run the project's full suite through the default invocation path.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
    [not_wired] only-named-tests-run/plugin-path-not-on-pytest-import-path
      The copied plugin module is written into `workspace/plugin`, but `_sandbox_env` only sets `PYTHONPATH` to that directory and the subprocess is launched from `project_root`; if pytest does not import the copied module from that path, the `-p _acceptance_sandbox_netblock` hook never loads and pytest can fall back to its normal collection behavior, including the full suite.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0

  single-test-time-budget
    [scope_too_narrow] single-test-time-budget/per-test-alarm-not-armed-on-unsupported-platforms
      The per-test timeout is skipped entirely when the platform lacks `signal.SIGALRM` or `signal.setitimer`, so a single test can run past its allotted budget on those interpreters.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [not_wired] single-test-time-budget/timeout-hook-misses-test-body-on-nonstandard-pytest-flow
      The timeout is installed in `pytest_runtest_protocol`, but if pytest executes a test path that does not go through that hook wrapper, the individual test budget is never armed and the test can overrun without being stopped.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [other] single-test-time-budget/alarm-handler-not-restored-after-interrupted-test
      The timeout alarm is only disarmed in the `finally` block around the hook wrapper; if the hook is bypassed or the signal state is altered by pytest or a plugin, a later test may inherit the wrong alarm state and the single-test budget can fail to apply cleanly to the intended test.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0

